1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,464
Good afternoon.

2
00:00:01,464 --> 00:00:02,460
Can I help you?

3
00:00:02,460 --> 00:00:03,363
Good afternoon.

4
00:00:03,363 --> 00:00:07,427
Yes, I've just transferred to the School of Education,

5
00:00:07,427 --> 00:00:11,040
and I'd like information about joining the library.

6
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:15,752
Well, the School of Education has libraries on two sites, as I'm sure you

7
00:00:15,752 --> 00:00:16,088
know.

8
00:00:16,088 --> 00:00:18,444
This one here is the Fordham Site,

9
00:00:18,444 --> 00:00:20,800
and the other is on Castle Road.

10
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:24,850
And is there any difference between the two libraries?

11
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:27,310
Not in terms of their facilities.

12
00:00:27,310 --> 00:00:32,230
Access to online databases and the Internet is available at both sites

13
00:00:32,230 --> 00:00:36,740
and each site has a range of reference materials on education.

14
00:00:36,740 --> 00:00:37,715
Oh yes.

15
00:00:37,715 --> 00:00:38,690
I see.

16
00:00:38,690 --> 00:00:43,788
But the Castle Road site has books on the sociology of education

17
00:00:43,788 --> 00:00:49,736
and a collection of textbooks and teaching resources covering most of the subjects taught

18
00:00:49,736 --> 00:00:51,010
in secondary schools.

19
00:00:51,010 --> 00:00:51,769
Ah, right,

20
00:00:51,769 --> 00:00:57,083
but I'm training to be a primary teacher so I need to look at

21
00:00:57,083 --> 00:01:00,120
materials for the five to eleven age group.

22
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:02,545
Then you've come to the right place.

23
00:01:02,545 --> 00:01:07,395
At Fordham we hold material relating to primary education, as well as special needs,

24
00:01:07,395 --> 00:01:12,244
but of course you'll need to familiarise yourself with both sites to make the

25
00:01:12,244 --> 00:01:13,630
most of our resources.

26
00:01:13,630 --> 00:01:15,990
You haven't mentioned periodicals.

27
00:01:15,990 --> 00:01:19,530
Are they held at both sites?

28
00:01:19,530 --> 00:01:23,993
Current issues, yes but if you want to look at back issues you'll need

29
00:01:23,993 --> 00:01:27,500
to use the CD-ROM databases which are held here at Fordham.

30
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:28,280
I see.

31
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:29,840
Now, about borrowing books.

32
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:31,790
I'm living out of town,

33
00:01:31,790 --> 00:01:35,690
so I'm hoping I can borrow quite a few items,

34
00:01:35,690 --> 00:01:40,370
and cut down on the number of trips I have to make.

35
00:01:40,370 --> 00:01:44,630
Right, well, members can borrow two books at a time from each site.

36
00:01:44,630 --> 00:01:46,370
Only two books?

37
00:01:46,370 --> 00:01:48,292
Only two from each site,

38
00:01:48,292 --> 00:01:49,830
but that's four altogether.

39
00:01:49,830 --> 00:01:50,739
Oh I see,

40
00:01:50,739 --> 00:01:53,770
and how long can I hang on to them for?

41
00:01:53,770 --> 00:01:55,917
The borrowing period is one month,

42
00:01:55,917 --> 00:01:58,422
but of course books can be renewed.

43
00:01:58,422 --> 00:02:02,000
You can renew any item a maximum of three times.

44
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,820
Do I have to come to the library to do that?

45
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:07,944
No, you can do it by telephone or email,

46
00:02:07,944 --> 00:02:11,415
but you can't renew overdue books this way, only before

47
00:02:11,415 --> 00:02:14,538
or on the due date stamped in the book.

48
00:02:14,538 --> 00:02:19,397
We'll need your full name, your borrower number and the name of the site

49
00:02:19,397 --> 00:02:21,480
library you borrowed the items from.

50
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:27,397
So theoretically I can borrow books for up to three months-is that what you're

51
00:02:27,397 --> 00:02:27,820
saying?

52
00:02:27,820 --> 00:02:30,000
Yes, provided they're not recalled.

53
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,420
So, what happens then?

54
00:02:32,420 --> 00:02:37,139
Well, sometimes an item is requested by another borrower, in which case we'd send

55
00:02:37,139 --> 00:02:38,150
you a letter,

56
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:41,858
and you'd have to return the book within seven working days.

57
00:02:41,858 --> 00:02:43,880
Don't forget we're closed on Sundays.

58
00:02:43,880 --> 00:02:44,660
OK.

59
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:49,114
I expect it'll take me a while to find what I need.

60
00:02:49,114 --> 00:02:50,970
There's such a lot here.

61
00:02:50,970 --> 00:02:52,112
Yes there is,

62
00:02:52,112 --> 00:02:57,439
but if you need help getting started, this term we're running three study skills

63
00:02:57,439 --> 00:02:57,820
workshops.

64
00:02:57,820 --> 00:02:58,282
Oh?

65
00:02:58,282 --> 00:03:00,130
What are they on?

66
00:03:00,130 --> 00:03:02,017
Er, let me see.

67
00:03:02,017 --> 00:03:05,791
The first one's on resources-yes, here it is.

68
00:03:05,791 --> 00:03:08,622
How to use the library's resources.

69
00:03:08,622 --> 00:03:13,340
That includes everything, not just the print and technical resources.

70
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:14,678
That sounds useful.

71
00:03:14,678 --> 00:03:17,800
Is there anything on using the Internet?

72
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:19,296
Er, let's see.

73
00:03:19,296 --> 00:03:24,281
The one on the Internet for beginners was last term.

74
00:03:24,281 --> 00:03:27,770
This term it's finding research materials online.

75
00:03:27,770 --> 00:03:32,760
That sounds interesting too, hm, what's the last one?

76
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:35,420
It's a workshop on dissertations.

77
00:03:35,420 --> 00:03:38,800
What do you mean-how to write one?

78
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:43,961
Er, no it's more to do with academic writing conventions, you know-writing a bibliography

79
00:03:43,961 --> 00:03:47,278
and how to refer to sources in your text.

80
00:03:47,278 --> 00:03:48,752
That sort of thing.

81
00:03:48,752 --> 00:03:52,070
Anyway, here's a leaflet with information about all three.

82
00:03:52,070 --> 00:03:53,437
Thanks a lot.

83
00:03:53,437 --> 00:03:55,260
That'll be very useful.

84
00:03:55,260 --> 00:03:55,680
You're welcome.
